The == operator is used for comparing objects or instances of classes. It returns true if two objects are equal, meaning they have the same value at the same time. For example:

String str1 = "Hello";
String str2 = "Hello";

System.out.println(str1.equals(str2)); // Output: true

On the other hand, the equals method is used for comparing strings directly. It compares the contents of the string object itself, without checking any additional properties like length or case sensitivity. The output will always be false because the contents of the string object are different:

String str3 = "hello";
String str4 = "Hello";

System.out.println(str3.equals(str4)); // Output: false

In summary, == is good for comparing objects or instances of classes, while equals is better suited for comparing strings directly.

